{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 50, :comments ["<b>And he shall reckon with his buyer.</b> Scripture warns that one must be precise with him in calculation and not come at him deviously, because robbing a gentile is forbidden on account of desecration of the Name, and it is of a gentile who is under one's hand that Scripture speaks, and even so the Torah warned about him that one must be careful not to rob him. And so our Rabbis, of blessed memory, said, and so they concluded in the Gemara: interest from a gentile is permitted, as it is said (Deuteronomy 23:21) לנכרי תשיך, and it is written (ibid.) לא תשיך לאחיך — 'your brother' and not a Cuthean; but to deceive him is forbidden, for behold there is in this desecration of HaShem, and if one returned his error to him, behold this is sanctification of HaShem. And on account of this Jacob commanded his sons to return the error of the gentile, as it is written (Genesis 43:12): ואת הכסף המושב בפי אמתחותיכם תשיבו בידכם — and even though the Egyptians were idol worshippers, he commanded to return it because of sanctification of HaShem, and this was before the giving of the Torah, and all the more so in this time that a person is obligated in this. His robbery is forbidden from what is written (Deuteronomy 7:16): ואכלת את כל העמים אשר ה' אלהיך נותן לך — at the time of the conquest of the Land of Israel, when they were delivered into your hand; and further from this verse: וחשב עם קונהו — that this one who redeems the slave must be precise with the gentile, his master, in calculation according to his years, for if he was not precise and robbed from him, there is in the matter desecration of HaShem more than one who robs an Israelite. And so we find in the Tosefta of Bava Kamma: one who robs a gentile is obligated to return it; the robbery of a gentile is more severe than the robbery of an Israelite because of desecration of HaShem — end quote. The reason for this is that one who robs his fellow Israelite does not raise an outcry and does not cast aspersion on the faith of Israel, but one who robs a gentile raises an outcry and casts aspersion on the faith of Israel and on the Torah of Moses, and behold this profanes HaShem. Joined to this you will find that Solomon, peace be upon him, prayed to the Holy One, blessed be He, that when an Israelite comes to pray in the House that he built for His Name, that He grant him his request according to what He knows is fitting for it — this is what he said (I Kings 8:39): אשר תדע את לבבו; but if the stranger comes there to pray, that He grant him his request in any case — this is what he said (ibid.): ועשית ככל אשר יקרא אליך הנכרי — that is, whether fitting or not fitting, give it to him; and the reason is because the Israelite does not raise an outcry and the stranger raises an outcry."], :book :leviticus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:28:36.035353199Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}